"H2436":"embrace","H3947":"take","H5186":"to stretch","H734":"way","H4941":"judgement","H854":"with","H7097":"end","H776":"country/planet","H3708":"vexation","H4470":"bitterness","H6064":"to fine","H3476":"uprightness","H2820":"to withhold","H561":"word","H3045":"to know","H1847":"knowledge","H7119":"cool","H3368":"precious","H8394":"understanding","H191":"fool(ish)","H2790":"to plow/plot","H2450":"wise","H2803":"design","H331":"to shutter"},"ccc":{"6":[2219]},"cccp":{"2219":"__P7U#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Victims. Of which part was used for a feast, chap. vii. 14. (Calmet) — Septuagint add, “of many goods, and unjust victims.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Brethren. Partaking with them, (Abenezra; Tirinus; Menochius) or rather acting as his master’s executor; which evinces the advantages of wisdom, so as to raise a slave above those whom his station requires him to serve. Such was Joseph, who was tried."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Lips. They mutually deceive one another, as well as others."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Fathers. Whose virtues they are bound to imitate."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Eloquent. Septuagint, “faithful.” They cannot be expected from them."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Expecteth heavenly things. Septuagint, “instruction is the reward of good deeds to those who use it.”"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Friends. Detractors cause dissensions, and even wars."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Fool. “A word is enough for the wise.” Nobilis equus umbra virgæ regitur, ignavus ne calcari quidem. (Q. Curt.)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Angel. Messenger of death, (Menochius) which is sometimes inflicted by good, at others by bad, angels of death, or satan, 2 Corinthians xii. 7., and Psalm lxxvii. 49."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Fool. Hebrew, “fool in his folly.” The danger is not greater in meeting (Calmet) a female bear, though it be the most terrible. (Aristotle, Anim. ix. 1.)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Water. It is scarcely possible to prevent the bad effects of detraction. Frequent quarrels have also risen on account of springs, Genesis xxvi. 20. — Judgment. And gives up the cause, as being much more prudent, Matthew v. 25, 40."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","God. We ought to suspend our judgment, or incline to the more favourable side, Matthew vii. 1., and Romans ii. 1."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","He, &c., is not here in Hebrew, though it be equivalently (ver. 19.) where the Septuagint are silent. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Distress. Like the real friend, chap. xviii. 24."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Hands. Through joy, or as a mark of his consent."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Door. Sixtus V reads, “mouth:” as some explain the door to mean. A large door supposes that the house is elevated, and thus exposed to danger from winds, &c."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Earth. Wandering and insatiable, and deeming the acquisition of wisdom too difficult."],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","No good. But very criminal. See Jeremias vii. 31."],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Precious and reserved spirit. This is a mark of wisdom. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}